# Sins of Regret — An Adventure in the Forgotten Heart of the Empire

**Version:** 0.4
**Source:** Legend of the Five Rings Roleplaying Game — Sins of Regret (product L5R11)
**Publisher:** Fantasy Flight Games (2020)

> GM-facing narrative. Boxed **read-aloud** text is reproduced verbatim; intervening GM procedure
> (check TNs, momentum thresholds, branch bookkeeping) is summarized faithfully — the full detail
> lives in the source PDF. The Dramatis Personae statblocks are in `l5r5e-0.4-sins-of-regret-cast.ttrpg`
> and the part structure in `l5r5e-0.4-sins-of-regret.arc`. Designed for rōnin PCs of rank 2+, this
> adventure uses the players' own characters, so there are no pregens. Poster maps of Twin Blessings
> Village and Closed Shell Castle ship with the boxed product and are referenced, not reproduced.

---

## Storyline Background

The adventure is set in and around **Twin Blessings Village** (introduced in *Path of Waves*), a rural, remote place far from any major holding where the word of the local daimyō is law — as long as he can enforce it. The villagers' main problems are an **elemental imbalance** (which has ruined the rice crop and turned the river erratic and barren) and their unsympathetic daimyō, **Reju Jikai**.

The villagers appeal — directly or through the PCs — for aid. Once the PCs reach **Closed Shell Castle** and gain an audience with the daimyō, they find he neither heeds their advice nor swears them to his service until they earn his good opinion. They may then seek to earn his trust or to remove him from power. Either way, Jikai's enemies move against him, and the nature of the **Final Conflict** reflects the decisions the PCs have made. In the end, Jikai either defeats his enemies or falls; the PCs might fight for or against him, and their success or failure determines not only their own future but that of everyone under his governorship.

The imbalance is not natural: Jikai's shugenja, **Reju Toshio**, has deliberately angered the kami — at the suggestion of the ambitious steward **Otomo Nobu** — to undermine the very lord he should be helping, a serious spiritual crime against the land itself.

---

## Part One: Twin Blessings Village

On the road the PCs may meet the amiable pilgrim **Naoki**, who wishes to reach the village shrine safely but fears wolves and bandits (and who tends to pilfer things he shouldn't). The village is a place of fear and want. Its leader is **Setsuo**; the fiercer **Natsumi** would rather the village fight back. The priest **Daizō** prays helplessly at the river shrine, unable to invoke the kami and haunted by a vision of his dead friend **Kokoro**.

Village locations show the rot: a once-beautiful **bridge** hurriedly repaired and hung with apologetic prayer ribbons; a **Shrine to the River Kami** where the erratic, destructive river reflects Toshio's offense; **secret caches** of weapons and sake the villagers hide from the daimyō; and, in the forest, the **Hermitage**, where the monk **Michi** once stayed and where **Ichirō** — Setsuo's son — now leads rebel bandits, claiming to have joined the **Perfect Land Sect** and hoping to overthrow Jikai.

### The Way Station

A burnt, looted husk. Lord Jikai ordered his bushi to shut it down, but the bushi **Rin** took her orders further — trapping everyone inside and burning them alive, while the guilt-wracked **Genzo** did not stop her. Among the victims was **Miya Emon**, a shugenja of an Imperial family on his way to visit his niece **Miya Shinako** at Closed Shell Castle; the elemental imbalance prevented him from quenching the flames. His ancestral **wakizashi** survived and must be returned to his family, or it brings ill fortune (an Affliction of the Void ring). The villager **Shinichi** witnessed the atrocity; a golden ring hidden in the stable is his.

### Collecting Taxes

The magistrate arrives early to collect a tax the village cannot pay:

> Three riders enter the village, their horses at a walk. The leader sits stiffly upright, his eyes narrowing above his mempō as he surveys the village. His purple armor is polished to a shine, rattling slightly with the movement of his horse. Behind him, his bushi are silent and alert. Their presence draws villagers from the fields and houses, who prostrate themselves on the dusty ground. Only Setsuo dares raise his head to speak: "Welcome, Lord Tsugumasa!"

**Reju Tsugumasa** wants the ledger (which Setsuo claims is lost) and to inspect the village. The PCs may intercede — a TN 3 Courtesy check buys the villagers time; producing or revealing the caches earns the magistrate's favor but loses the villagers'. If the PCs don't intervene, Setsuo is arrested and Natsumi takes charge, preparing a revolt. Either leader then makes **A Plea for Help**, and the village argues whether to flee, appeal to the lord, or rise up.

---

## Part Two: Closed Shell Castle

The road to the castle runs through **Imbalanced Terrain**:

> The road stretches through fields, then forest. Throughout the relatively short journey, it is clear something is wrong with the land itself. The wind rises and threatens to knock you off course one moment, then falls still the next. The sun beats down hard, then without warning clouds gather and unleash a torrent of freezing rain, chilling you to the bone. Hopelessness pervades your soul, an unshakable sense of foreboding and terrible doom ahead…

Near the castle the PCs meet **Michi**, friendly and cryptic on the road but grave and silent once inside, quoting Shinsei and waiting for something he will not name.

### Meeting the Daimyō

**Reju Jikai** — once an idealist, now bitter and exhausted — withholds his trust and his service until the PCs earn his good opinion. He suffers the Exhausted condition he cannot shake, thanks to the nightingale floor and the strange noises on the roof his yōjimbō **Jinzaburō** could never explain. The PCs may ingratiate themselves by reconciling him with his wife (**Family Matters**), solving his sleeping problems (the rooftop), recovering the missing arrows, or uncovering the truth of his downfall; two such deeds earn his trust and an invitation to hunt with his falcon **Toppi**.

### The Castle's Sins

- **The Conspiracy:** the steward **Otomo Nobu** ran the castle before Jikai's appointment and means to be daimyō in his place. He has enlisted the shugenja **Reju Toshio** — who worsens the imbalance for promised wealth and power — and has spread his influence through the servants.
- **The Well & the Kyōkotsu:** Nobu murdered **Otomo Nahoko** and threw her bones down the well; her vengeful **kyōkotsu** now inhabits it, and the servants (including **Shaki** and the frightened **Atsuko**) cannot draw water. The bones must be retrieved and cremated with proper funerary rites (a Cleansing Rite) for her spirit to rest.
- **The Rooftop & the Shachihoko:** the roof's guardian carp-tigers, the **shachihoko**, neglected by Toshio and inflamed by the turbulence, have grown paranoid and attack intruders — the source of the noises that plague Jikai's sleep.
- **Family Matters:** **Seppun Azusa**, Jikai's wife, has withdrawn into obsessive devotion to Shinsei since a hand injury ended her martial life; teaching her to fight left-handed and reconciling her with Jikai lifts them both. Their niece and heir **Reju Ume**, a duelist trained by **Kakita Chisato** in Otosan Uchi, will speak to Jikai for any PC who bests her in an iaijutsu duel; **Miya Shinako** writes haiku in the dead garden.

### The Rival Daimyō

**Otomo Itsuki**, who holds no respect for Jikai, ignores Nobu's overtures — but the steward has been working through **Otomo Osamu**'s representative **Otomo Niko** to have troops sent to aid his coup, in exchange for keeping his stewardship when he takes the castle.

---

## Part Three: The Final Conflict

Jikai must defend himself within the castle — from an invading force, traitors within, or both. The climactic mass battle is run as cohorts led by the PCs, with the possible armies reflecting the PCs' choices:

- **Osamu's Army** — well-trained bushi (discipline 40, strength 50), Tactically Trained, commanded by **Otomo Kazumi**.
- **Bandit Band** — inexperienced (discipline 20, strength 45), commanded by **Ichirō**.
- **Village Mob** — commanded by **Natsumi**.
- **Jikai's Army** — commanded by **Jikai** (or another, if he is persuaded to step aside).

**A Traitor at the Gates:** if Jikai keeps the gate closed, a traitor within (Nobu, or Michi) opens it. Whether Jikai's archers can then punish the attackers depends on whether the lost arrows were recovered. **The Enemy Within:** if no external force is in position, the Final Conflict still begins at nightfall with Nobu's mercenaries turning on Jikai's loyalists:

> Fighting erupts in the barracks, and the cries of the dying and the clash of steel echo through the growing darkness. Someone rings the alarm bell, and the daimyō himself emerges from the keep, despite the protestations of his yōjimbō. He wants to see for himself what new trouble afflicts his castle…

**Out for Themselves:** optional encounters let PCs who won't pick a side still feel the climax — saving courtiers driven out by fire, confronting **Rin** and her mercenaries, catching a corrupt character fleeing (Masaru, Atsuko, Toshio, or Nobu), or aiding **Reju Daihachi** as he tries to spirit Ume to safety through the derelict tower.

---

## Conclusion & Rewards

The ending depends on the PCs' choices. Helping Jikai keep his seat may see them sworn into his service — rōnin no longer, and +2 glory if that was their goal. Saving the village — by replacing or reforming the daimyō, or by resolving the elemental imbalance — earns an increase of 3 or more to both honor and glory, and a legend told in the village for generations. Even PCs who fail may have learned from their mistakes. XP scales with how many problems they tackled and how well they balanced ninjō and duty.

---

## Ties to Path of Waves & Cresting Waves

Twin Blessings Village is drawn from *Path of Waves: The Essential Guide to Rōnin*, and this adventure can be a return visit that exposes the changes a new daimyō has wrought. Further encounters in and around the village and Closed Shell Castle continue in the downloadable **Cresting Waves**. The boxed product's poster maps of both locations hold many details left open for the GM to develop.
